---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import line from "../data/line.json";
const shown = line.filter(g => !g.living);
---
<Base title="The direct line">
  <h1>The direct line</h1>
  <p class="lede">Nine generations from the Arienzo of the 1730s to Brisbane. Each row says what the
  evidence is, and each generation is joined to the one above it by a record that names a father.
  The ninth is living and is not published here; eight are shown.</p>

  <div class="spine">
    {shown.map(g => (
      <div class={"gen" + (g.gen === 1 ? " undoc" : "")}>
        <div><span class="yr">{g.gen}</span><span class="who">{g.name}</span></div>
        <div class="whr">
          {g.born ? "b. " + g.born : ""}
          {g.died ? " · d. " + g.died : ""}
          {g.bornPlace ? " · " + g.bornPlace : ""}
          {g.spouse ? " · m. " + g.spouse : ""}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p class="spine-key">A solid ring is a generation joined to the one above it by a record that names
  a father. <strong>Matteo's ring is open</strong>: no record has been found that names <em>his</em>
  father, and the line stops there. That gap is the point of the drawing.</p>

  {shown.map(g => (
    <section class="card" style="margin:1rem 0">
      <h3 style="margin-bottom:.35rem">
        <span class="mono" style="color:var(--ink-3)">{g.gen}.</span> {g.name}
        {" "}<span class={"chip " + (g.confidence === "documented" ? "doc" : "dis")}>{g.confidence}</span>
      </h3>
      <p class="small" style="margin:.1rem 0 .6rem">
        b. {g.born}{g.bornPlace ? ", " + g.bornPlace : ""}
        {g.died ? " · d. " + g.died + (g.diedPlace ? ", " + g.diedPlace : "") : ""}
        {g.spouse ? " · m. " + g.spouse : ""}
        {g.spouseBorn ? " (b. " + g.spouseBorn + ")" : ""}
        {g.married ? " · " + g.married : ""}
        {g.trade ? " · " + g.trade : ""}
      </p>
      <p style="margin:0">{g.note}</p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>The welds</h2>
  <p>A pedigree is only as good as the joins. Three records carry this one:</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Date</th><th>What it says</th><th>Joins</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Death of Vincenzo Falco</td><td>15 May 1875, Arienzo</td>
        <td>Names his father as <strong>Pasquale</strong></td><td>gen 3 → gen 2</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Death of Raffaele Falco</td><td>21 Apr 1880, Arienzo</td>
        <td>Names his father as <strong>Vincenzo</strong></td><td>gen 4 → gen 3</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Birth of Carmine Antonio Falco</td><td>10 Jul 1850, Arienzo</td>
        <td>Names his father as <strong>Raffaele</strong></td><td>gen 5 → gen 4</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Death of Pasquale Falco</strong></td><td>1 Oct 1834, Arienzo</td>
        <td>Names his parents as <strong>Matteo Falco</strong> and <strong>Francesca Crisci</strong>,
        and his wife as <strong>Chiara Rivetti</strong></td><td>gen 2 → gen 1</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small">Generation 2 is now welded upward twice over: by Pasquale's baptism of 1767 in the
  parish register, and independently by his civil death act of 1834, which names the same two parents
  from a different archive and a different kind of record. <strong>Above Matteo there is no weld.</strong>
  His marriage to Francesca Crisci would name his father; it was searched for across 2,056 transcribed
  pages and not found, and his own baptism falls in the gap the register leaves between 1738 and 1761.
  That is the frontier of this research. See <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Open questions</a>.</p>
</Base>
